<Prose title="Support" description="Answers to common questions about HostDeck, and how to get help.">
  <p>Answers to the questions people ask most. Can’t find yours? <a href={site.supportUrl}>Ask on GitHub</a>.</p>

  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>How do I open HostDeck?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Click its icon in the menu bar (a small stack of cards), or press <kbd>⌃⌥⌘S</kbd> from any app. You can change
          the shortcut, or turn it off, in Settings › General. In the menu, start typing to search, and press Return to
          open the top result.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Where are my hosts saved?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>In a single file on your Mac, with automatic backups next to it in a <code>Backups</code> folder:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Direct download: <code>~/Library/Application Support/HostDeck/hosts.json</code></li>
          <li>
            Mac App Store:{' '}
            <code>~/Library/Containers/net.othi.hostdeck/Data/Library/Application Support/HostDeck/hosts.json</code>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>Settings › Config File shows the file, and can import or export it.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>I used Shuttle. Can I bring my hosts?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Yes. The direct download imports <code>~/.shuttle.json</code> on first launch, keeping Shuttle’s ⌥⌘S shortcut.
          In the Mac App Store version, use Settings › Config File › Import and choose the file. The format stays
          Shuttle’s, so the file still works with Shuttle too.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Why does Terminal ask before connecting?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          The Mac App Store version opens SSH hosts as ssh:// links, and Terminal asks you to confirm links from other
          apps when the host isn’t in <code>~/.ssh/known_hosts</code>. Click Allow. iTerm2 doesn’t ask; you can switch to
          it in Settings › General. With a Terminal Script attached (see below), and in the direct download, HostDeck
          runs the command in Terminal itself, so there’s no question.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Can the Mac App Store version run any command, not just SSH hosts?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Yes, with an optional Terminal Script: an AppleScript you attach that decides how HostDeck opens commands.
          HostDeck comes with an example for Terminal and one for iTerm2, as plain text you can read and change.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Choose Settings… from the HostDeck menu and open the Access tab.</li>
          <li>Click Open Scripts Folder, then drag the example shown in Settings (it matches your terminal) into that folder.</li>
          <li>The first time a command runs, macOS asks whether HostDeck may control your terminal. Click OK.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          From then on every command runs in Terminal or iTerm2, with the profile and title you chose. iTerm2 opens new
          tabs; Terminal opens new windows, because it has no scripting command for tabs. To stop using the script, move
          it from the Scripts folder to the Bin.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>How do I show the hosts from my ~/.ssh/config?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Turn on “Show hosts from ~/.ssh/config” in Settings › General. The Mac App Store version asks you once to allow
          access to your <code>.ssh</code> folder.
        </p>
        <p>
          To organise them, add a comment on a line under a <code>Host</code> line: <code># hostdeck.name Folder/Name</code> puts
          the host in a folder, and <code># hostdeck.environment prod</code> labels it Production.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>How do Production labels work?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          In Manage Hosts, give a host or a whole folder the Production, Staging or Development label. Labelled hosts get
          a coloured dot. In Settings › General › Production hosts, choose what happens before a Production host opens:
          ask to confirm, require Touch ID or your password, or nothing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Which apps open SFTP and FTP hosts?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          In Manage Hosts, choose Type › File transfer and enter the server. HostDeck opens it in Transmit, ForkLift,
          Cyberduck, Mountain Duck, Commander One or FileZilla, or Finder for FTP downloads. Pick one in Settings ›
          General › File transfer, or let HostDeck choose. To browse an SSH host’s files, hold ⌥ on it in the menu and choose
          Browse Files (SFTP).
        </p>
        <p>
          The free FileZilla client doesn’t accept links from other apps, so the direct download starts FileZilla with the
          address, and the Mac App Store version copies the link for FileZilla’s Quickconnect bar.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>What is HostDeck Pro, and how do I cancel it?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          An optional subscription in the Mac App Store version, with a free trial: workspaces, server health with
          maintenance windows, uptime reports, SSH security, cloud import, on-device AI (on Macs with Apple
          Intelligence), command templates, Production rules, SSH keys, history and Shortcuts actions. Everything else
          in HostDeck is free.
        </p>
        <p>
          To manage or cancel it, open the App Store, click your name, then Account Settings › Subscriptions › Manage, or
          choose Manage Subscription in HostDeck’s Settings › HostDeck Pro. It ends at the end of the period you paid for.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>How do command templates work? (HostDeck Pro)</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Put blanks in a host’s command, such as <code>ssh {'{{'}user{'}}'}@{'{{'}host{'}}'}</code>,
          or <code>{'{{'}cluster:prod,staging{'}}'}</code> to offer a list. When the host opens, HostDeck asks for the
          values once, shows the command as it will run, and quotes each value, so it stays one value: it can’t add
          commands or options. Recent values are offered next time; blanks named like a password, secret or token are
          typed hidden and never remembered.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>What does the connection log keep? (HostDeck Pro)</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Nothing until you turn it on in Settings › Production. Then, for Production hosts (or all hosts), the time, the
          host’s name and label, its saved command, the reason you typed, and whether it opened, was cancelled or was
          blocked by a change freeze. It’s a file next to your hosts file (<code>Connections/log.csv</code>), on your Mac
          only. Insights › Connection Log shows it, exports it as CSV and clears it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Why does HostDeck ask for Local Network access? (HostDeck Pro)</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Server Health connects to the hosts you chose to check, and macOS asks before an app connects to devices on
          your local network. Without it, those hosts show “Local Network access is off”, never “down”. You can change it
          in System Settings › Privacy &amp; Security › Local Network.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>What does the on-device AI do, and what does it see? (HostDeck Pro)</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          It finds a host you describe (“the billing staging database”), writes an ssh command from a sentence (“follow
          the nginx log on the web servers”), and says what a saved command does before it opens. It uses Apple
          Intelligence, on macOS 26 or later on a Mac that supports it, and runs entirely on your Mac: what you type and
          your hosts are never sent anywhere.
        </p>
        <p>
          What it writes can be wrong, so HostDeck shows it for you to check and change, never runs or saves it by
          itself, and never offers a command that could erase a server. The facts about a command (where ssh connects,
          what each option does, what changes or deletes things) come from HostDeck itself. Turn it off in Settings ›
          HostDeck Pro, or turn on Apple Intelligence in System Settings to use it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>How do maintenance windows work? (HostDeck Pro)</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Pause a host’s or a folder’s alerts for an hour, four hours or until tomorrow morning, or schedule windows once
          or every week, such as Sundays from 02:00 to 04:00, in Server Health’s Maintenance… During a window HostDeck
          keeps checking, so it knows when the host is back, but doesn’t tell you it went down, and the menu bar and
          widgets don’t count it. If the host is still down when the window ends, you’re told then.
        </p>
        <p>
          Uptime reports leave the time out: outages during a window are listed as planned maintenance, not downtime.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>How do uptime reports work? (HostDeck Pro)</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          From Server Health’s history, HostDeck makes a report of your checked hosts for last month, this month so far,
          the last 30 days or dates you choose: for all of them or one folder, with who it’s for and who prepared it if
          you send it to a client. Save it as a PDF, with a page for each host and its outages, or as CSV files for a
          spreadsheet. It can also save last month’s report to a folder by itself on the 1st of each month.
        </p>
        <p>
          Uptime is the share of checks a host answered, and an outage lasts from its first failed check to the first
          answered one. HostDeck checks only while your Mac is awake and online, so every report says how much of the
          period was monitored, and a sleeping Mac never counts as downtime.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>What does SSH security check, and what does it send? (HostDeck Pro)</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Once a day, for the SSH hosts that Server Health checks, HostDeck reads what each server shows before login:
          its version (such as OpenSSH 9.6p1), its host key and the algorithms it offers. Then it says goodbye. It never
          logs in, and sends no user name, password or key.
        </p>
        <p>
          It compares the host key with your <code>~/.ssh/known_hosts</code> file (in the Mac App Store version, once
          you allow access to your <code>.ssh</code> folder), or with the key it saw before, and the version with a list
          of known OpenSSH vulnerabilities that comes with HostDeck. Debian and Ubuntu name their package in the
          version, so fixes they installed count; on other systems a vulnerable version is shown as possibly affected
          until you check the server’s updates and ignore it. What it finds stays on your Mac.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Which token does cloud import need, and where is it kept? (HostDeck Pro)</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          A read-only one: HostDeck only lists your servers.
          For DigitalOcean, a token with the <code>droplet:read</code> scope (and <code>tag:read</code> for tags);
          for Hetzner Cloud, a project token with Read permission;
          for Tailscale, an OAuth client with <code>devices:core:read</code> (it never expires) or an API access token.
        </p>
        <p>
          The token stays in the keychain of the Mac you typed it on: never in iCloud Keychain, your hosts file or a
          backup. HostDeck sends it only to the provider’s own API. Removing the account in Cloud Accounts deletes it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Can HostDeck show only my work hosts during my Work Focus?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Yes. In System Settings › Focus, choose a Focus, then Focus Filters › Add Filter › HostDeck, and pick the
          folders and labels to show. While that Focus is on, the menu, the Quick Launcher, the main window and the
          widgets show only those hosts. Nothing is moved or changed, the main window’s Show All shows every host, and
          Server Health keeps checking all of them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Does HostDeck store my passwords?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          No. ssh signs in with your keys or asks for a password itself, and file-transfer apps keep passwords in their
          own keychain. See the <a href={url('privacy/')}>privacy policy</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>

  <div class="contact">
    <h2>Still stuck?</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={site.supportUrl}>Open an issue on GitHub</a>. Mention your macOS version and HostDeck version (HostDeck menu
      › About HostDeck), and which edition you use.
    </p>
  </div>
</Prose>
